s you're talking about  we talked about job cants relocations  but we've got people cancelling  investment we have one company in the  West Midlands for example contacted us  last week that is going to mothball its  UK operation because its investors and  owners want to quote move to a more  stable country that's where our politics  is left quite a lot of our businesses in  Kent for example right now companies  preparing for a situation where there's  total gridlock on the roads and people  can't even go about their day-to-day  business these are the kinds of things  happening in the real world right now  and to everyone in politics whether they  be in government or in Parliament  they need to get a grip of this very  quickly because these impacts are real  and they're out there you obviously have  a lot of conversations with ministers  with members of government with advisors  and so on are you confident that they  actually have a grip on what's happening  do they have a plan or they just feel in  their way  well I think they are certainly  listening to some of these concerns for  business but we have yet to see that  clear and concrete route forward I  looked at the Sunday newspapers this  morning and actually I was quite  depressed by what I saw because it was  all about the who rather than about the  what what we're concerned about at the  moment is what is that path forward what  is the time line that businesses are  going to have in order to adjust to a  new reality whatever that may be and  where are the practical answers to the  real world questions that they're  ringing us up and asking every single  day you mentioned this on their  newspapers of course they're full of  news of Coos plots against the Prime  Minister I mean do you think that would  be helpful to have a new prime minister  who might be able to find a way forward  or do you think that actually this is a  distraction and it could be worse well  when you have companies out there saying  to you I